<BaseLayout title="Methodology" description="The exact formulas and missing-data rules behind Tokenmaxx monthly coding-subscription comparisons.">
  <section class="page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Simple arithmetic, explicit denominators</p><h1>A plan is useful only when its model, allowance, and quality evidence line up.</h1><p class="lede">Tokenmaxx does not turn requests, agent credits, app-builder tokens, or “unlimited” marketing into model tokens without a defensible conversion.</p></section>
  <article class="prose section compact">
    <h2>Primary token calculation</h2>
    <pre class="formula"><code>{`blended API $/1M = fresh_share × fresh_input_rate
                    + cache_share × cache_read_rate
                    + output_share × output_rate

included raw tokens (M) = model-eligible monthly pool / blended API $/1M
subscription $/1M       = monthly subscription price / included raw tokens (M)`}</code></pre>
    <p>The standardized profile uses 20% fresh input, 70% cache reads, and 10% output. The agentic profile uses 2.594% fresh input, 97.108% cache reads, and 0.298% output from public coding-agent telemetry.</p>
    <h2>Quality-adjusted token cost</h2><pre class="formula"><code>{`quality-adjusted subscription $/1M
  = subscription $/1M / (external intelligence score / 100)`}</code></pre><p>This makes a billion tokens of a weak model worth less than a billion tokens of a stronger model while preserving the original price and score. It is a density metric, not a claim that intelligence is linear.</p>
    <h2>Task economics</h2><pre class="formula"><code>{`external API $/successful task = published API $/attempt / published pass rate
monthly task attempts           = included raw tokens / published raw tokens per task
monthly successful tasks        = attempts × published pass rate
subscription $/successful task  = subscription price / successful tasks`}</code></pre><p>Task results remain tied to the external benchmark and harness. They are plan-capacity counterfactuals, not promises that another agent harness reproduces the same pass rate.</p>
    <h2>Five comparison classes</h2><ul><li><strong>Token:</strong> a defensible model-specific token denominator.</li><li><strong>Request:</strong> numeric request weights and counts, but no stable tokens per request.</li><li><strong>Managed:</strong> platform credits, agent work units, app-builder tokens, daily tasks, or fair-use throughput.</li><li><strong>Relative:</strong> a multiplier is published, but the absolute base pool is not.</li><li><strong>Provider-hidden:</strong> the subscription exists, but the absolute allowance or deduction formula is withheld.</li></ul>
    <h2>Owned-plan behavior</h2><p>{guide.methodology.ownedPlanRule}</p>
    <h2>Missing data</h2><p>{guide.methodology.missingDataRule}</p><p>A provider-hidden record is a completed research result when the provider itself withholds the required number. It remains in the catalog with the exact missing fields and is excluded only from rankings that require that denominator.</p>
    <h2>Current model access exceptions</h2><p>Claude Fable 5 is PAYG-only on Claude Pro. Claude Max includes Fable but caps it at 50% of the shared weekly meter; because Anthropic does not publish the Fable meter multiplier, Tokenmaxx applies only the official share cap and keeps the remaining uncertainty visible.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
